      "enTitle": "Asking for Help in English",
      "words": 163,
      "text": "One of the most useful things I can learn in English is how to keep a conversation going when I do not understand. Instead of smiling and hoping the meaning becomes clear, I can ask a specific question. “Could you explain what you mean by that?” is useful when the words sound familiar but the message does not. If I miss a detail, I can check it: “Did you say Tuesday or Thursday?” I can also repeat the idea in my own words to make sure I have understood. These strategies do not hide the gaps in my English. They help me work with those gaps and stay involved. A good conversation does not require me to understand every word immediately. Clarification is also useful when speaking with a child. A short sentence, a gesture, or a familiar example may communicate more than a longer explanation. The purpose of language is to share meaning, not to demonstrate how many complicated words I know.",
