      "words": 162,
      "text": "Choosing a book is partly about interest and partly about difficulty. A book may be famous without being the right choice for me at the moment. Before committing to it, I read a page and check whether I can follow the main idea without constant help from a dictionary. If every sentence becomes a puzzle, I look for an easier starting point. When the level feels right, I read a short section before stopping to check important expressions. Then I try to explain the idea in plain English. For a story, I describe what happened. For a book about ideas, I identify the main point and think of an example. Understanding a book means more than recognizing the words on the page. An argument can be clearly written and still be incomplete. As I move toward books about judgment and personal development, I want to ask what evidence supports a claim, what assumptions it depends on, and when it might not apply.",
