      "enTitle": "Looking Back and Moving Forward",
      "words": 162,
      "text": "At the end of a month, it is tempting to judge progress by how many passages I have finished. That number matters less, however, if I cannot use the language outside my study time. A more useful question is whether I can explain something that I struggled to express before. Can I describe a difficult morning, ask a follow-up question, or give a reason for my opinion? I also want to notice what still feels uncertain. Perhaps I understand a sentence on paper but miss it when someone says it quickly. These details can guide my next steps. Looking back should help me make better decisions, rather than simply make me feel pleased or disappointed with a score. The strongest evidence of learning is transfer: using a familiar expression in an unfamiliar situation. Next month, I want to test this by changing the topic, answering an unexpected question, and explaining an idea without relying on the wording of the original passage.",
